**Theorem 8.1 (same-law operational transfer of the criterion).** Under
Assumptions 1.1 and 2.2, every finite law produced by a coordinate
face or the interpolation (FC.18), after the compatible merges,
has a common original optimal realization at its own request
$s=s_0r/\sqrt\alpha$, with the exact complete original outputs,
sharp joint cost and entire-fibre activity bound. For every integer
$m\ge0$ and every $r\in[r_-,r_+]$,

$$
\begin{gathered}
\mathcal T(s,t)=\frac{H_0+y\cdot B-\lambda r}{2},\qquad
n_{\rm tot}(s,t)=1+n_t(r),\\
n_{\rm tot}(s,t)\le m+1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r\in U_m,\\
n_{\rm tot}(s,t)=9\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r\in U_8\setminus U_7,
\qquad
n_{\rm tot}(s,t)=10\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r\notin U_8.
\end{gathered}
\tag{FC.24}
$$

The last equivalence is restricted to $r\in[r_-,r_+]$. If the
interval is nontrivial, its physical height slope is
$-\lambda\sqrt\alpha/(2s_0)$. There is no assertion of a slope
on an open interval when $r_-=r_+$.

Proof. The common moment equations on any such law give
$\sum p=1$, $\sum pu=\sum pv=\sum puv=0$ and
$\sum pu^2=\alpha$, hence $\sum pv^2=\beta$.
After $z=u/\sqrt\alpha$, $w=v/\sqrt\beta$ these are the
**complete** live Gram $\sum phh^T=I_3$, including all cross
entries. The last three equations give

$$
\sum pb_x=\mu,\qquad \sum pb_xz^2=V,\qquad
\sum pb_xzw=0,\qquad \sum pb_xw^2=W.
\tag{FC.25}
$$

The fourth follows from $\alpha z^2+\beta w^2=1$ and
$\mu=\alpha V+\beta W$. Thus
$\sum pb_x n_hn_h^T=M$: its lower diagonals are
$d^2W/h_2^2=m_1$ and $b^2V/h_1^2=m_2$, and its off-diagonal
vanishes. Pointwise $b_x=t\mathfrak c(h)\xi$ is legal physical
capacity. These are the GC Proposition 12.2 equations for one
joint law, not separately optimized marginal moment tables.
Merging preserved them all and preserved absolute height by
Theorem 3.3. Theorem 4.2 gives exact contrast and global optimality.

Apply the **same** GC Proposition 10.1 constructor to that finite
law. Each occupied low acquisition has supported live product
$\sqrt p K_h$ and failure effect $pb_xn_hn_h^T$. Its actual
effect includes both, namely
$pK_h^\dagger K_h+pb_xn_hn_h^T$; coefficient weight $p$ is
not the probability of acquisition. Rank two on every occupied
ellipse ray, including axes, is supplied there. The complete live
Gram gives its common live CP map on all complex inputs, preserving
coherences. The full $M$ gives each of the five original failure
Borel CP maps separately, including the zero-target ones.
Tensoring with arbitrary passive references retains their conditional
operators. These common maps preserve the complete twelve-history
stopped-cq HALF errors: the inherited minimax has the $5$ and $27$
errors equal to $E_*$ and the $16$ error strictly smaller, not
twelve equal scalar errors.

For specificity, the inherited chronological construction uses
$g(a)=2/(1+a)^2$, $G_{\rm time}(a)=2a/(1+a)$ and the low
failure operator $F_i$ on its entire qubit, with
$Z_i(a)=\sqrt{I-G_{\rm time}(a)F_i}$ and
$R_i(a)=g(a)F_i[I-G_{\rm time}(a)F_i]^{-1}$. Its integrated
rate norm is $\log(1+b_i/\mathfrak c(h_i))\le A$ and terminal
